harper: Repro: Java SDK sampleapp hitting ANR on emulator when retry jitter/backoff is enabled. Kotlin sample didn't ANR.

nina: Can you paste the logs / stack?

harper: Logs show main thread watchdog complaining after a bunch of scheduled retry tasks pile up: 

harper: ```
ANR in com.redwood.sampleapp (uid 10234): Input dispatching timed out (Waiting to send non-key event because the touched window has not finished processing certain input events.)
at java.lang.Thread.sleep(Native Method)
  at java.util.concurrent.ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor$DelayedWorkQueue.take(ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor.java:...)
  at java.util.concurrent.ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor$DelayedWorkQueue.take(ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor.java:...)
  at okhttp3.internal.concurrent.TaskRunner.execute(TaskRunner.kt:...)
```

rob: Wait, why is TaskRunner in the stack from scheduler.take? Are we blocking the scheduler thread with network calls that somehow interact with the OkHttp dispatcher?

harper: Short version: our Java retry backoff uses a ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor with corePoolSize=1 that runs blocking retry tasks which call synchronous Retrofit/OkHttp execute(). Under heavy burst and when the app posts micro-tasks to main thread from callbacks, we see the scheduler thread get saturated and Android's watchdog flags it as contributing to ANR. Kotlin sampleapp uses a coroutine-based backoff (Dispatchers.IO) which schedules work differently and doesn't block the single scheduler thread.

nina: Can you paste the Java snippet?

harper: ```java
// simplified Java retry scheduler in SDK-java
class ScheduledRetry {
  private final ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor sched = new ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor(1);

  void scheduleRetry(Runnable task, long delayMs) {
    sched.schedule(() -> {
      try {
        task.run(); // task performs blocking client.newCall(req).execute()
      } catch (Throwable t) {
        // log and maybe reschedule
      }
    }, delayMs, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
  }
}
```

harper: If many tasks are scheduled and the one thread is blocked on network IO, subsequent schedules queue up in the executor's queue. On Android the app ends up with a small number of background threads doing heavy IO while main thread waits on callbacks; combined with a tight watchdog this looked like an ANR in CI.

rob: The obvious fixes: 1) don't do blocking IO on ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor with pool size 1; 2) call sched.setRemoveOnCancelPolicy(true) and use a bounded queue; 3) prefer an async backoff implementation (ScheduledExecutor that posts to a larger worker pool) or, on Kotlin, coroutines with Dispatchers.IO.

nina: Also worth noting: ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor.schedule wraps tasks and can inherit caller thread's Interrupt state when run via execute; make sure we handle interruption and cancellation gracefully or tasks may hang.

harper: I prototyped two changes: A) change the scheduler to schedule a lightweight timer task that enqueues work onto a cached thread pool: `sched.schedule(() -> workerPool.submit(networkTask), delayMs, ...)` and B) for Java SDK, add a small backoff helper that uses the SDK's shared worker executor (with reasonable core threads) instead of doing network on scheduler thread. Both reduce pile-up.

rob: Can you add a sampleapp test that simulates burst + flaky server to catch this pattern? Also add a short note in the SDK migration doc: "Do not perform blocking network IO directly in ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor timer tasks; offload to worker pool or use coroutine-based delays."

harper: PR opened: https://git.internal/redwood/sdk-java/pull/276 - switches ScheduledRetry to offload to workerPool and sets removeOnCancel on the scheduler. Includes integration test that fires 200 simultaneous requests with jittered retry delays.

ci-bot: Created issue #4745: "Java SDK: scheduler-based retry does blocking IO on timer thread -> tasks pile up / ANR" - https://git.internal/redwood/issues/4745 :alarm_clock:

nina: Also add a quick sample in onboarding showing the safe pattern (schedule light timers + submit to worker pool) and a short comment in SDK README about using setRemoveOnCancelPolicy(true) when canceling scheduled retries.

